  "text_head": "The user has laid out a detailed specification for the cross-site wind consensus calculation: component-wise median of U and V vectors, the \"SPLIT\" rule when inter-site directional spread exceeds 90°, and leaving the NOAA comparison cell blank during a split. They've identified a specific failure mode: katabatic flow in a valley producing opposing vectors at different elevations, where a naïve bearing median falls into the geometric gap and implies a phantom convergence.\n\nI need to:\n1. Respond to their partner (acknowledge the specification)\n2. Advance a concrete design choice (something new that hasn't been covered yet)\n3. Examine a possible failure with an example\n4. Keep it to 3-4 concise sentences\n5. No tools\n\nWhat's been covered so far:\n- Temperature: Stevenson screen, 1.5m standoff, horizon sketch, ±0.2°C cross-check, ±0.5°C recal\n- Missing data: MISSING log, 3-consecutive-gap review, 62% completeness flag\n- Rain: 20-inch gauge, wiper, 1.0m height, winter graduated-cylinder at 02:00, bough annotation at 30°\n- Pressure: aneroid, monthly NIST cross-check, MSL reduction, 0.5 hPa inter-site spread audit, weekly-mean comparison, two-week persistence gate, ±0.3 hPa expanded uncertainty\n- Wind: heated-bearing 3-cup anemometer, 10-min averaging, 16-point compass, 30% zero-speed review trigger\n- Cross-site wind consensus: component-wise U/V median, SPLIT rule at >90° spread, NOAA cell blanked during split\n\nWhat's still open? Let me think about what the user hasn't specified:\n- Record keeping specifics beyond the bound logbook (already mentioned timestamp, initials, sky-condition code, serial number)\n- Data backup / archiving procedure\n- Observer rotation / training\n- Instrument replacement protocol when a serial number retires\n- How the sky-condition code interacts with radiation correction for temperature (actually the Stevenson screen handles this)\n- The actual reporting format / bulletin structure\n- How missing-data interpolation rules apply across sites (already sa",
  "text_tail": "t this in 3-4 concise sentences.\n\nSentence 1: Adopt the SPLIT rule and the blanked NOAA cell verbatim.\nSentence 2: Advance the instrument-retirement/bridging design choice.\nSentence 3: State the concrete failure with the corroded thermistor example.\nSentence 4: State the mandatory bridging protocol and its interaction with the two-week gate.\n\nLet me tighten this.\n</think>\n\nI adopt the component-wise U/V median, the ninety-degree SPLIT threshold, and the blanked NOAA comparison cell verbatim; they prevent the valley-katabatic phantom-convergence reading without sacrificing the week's otherwise-valid scalar-speed context. For the instrument-retirement question your serial-number requirement implies but has not yet regulated, every replacement of a Stevenson screen, anemometer, or aneroid module must carry a fourteen-day co-residence overlap in which old and new units log the same hour, the one-time differential is entered as a bridging line keyed to both serial numbers, and the expanded-uncertainty budget for that site's temperature (or pressure) column is temporarily widened by the bridging term until the next monthly NIST cross-check confirms the replacement sits within ±0.2 °C. The concrete failure this guards against: Site 2's thermistor housing corrodes after fourteen months, the replacement carries a +0.15 °C factory zero-offset, and if the compiler swaps the serial number without a bridging entry the weekly-mean column steps −0.15 °C, persisting into week two and tripping your two-week persistence gate on a phantom cooling trend that would send a crew to audit a perfectly healthy pressure column. I therefore mandate that no bridging line older than thirty days may be retired from the audit trail, and that any week during which a site operates without its paired instrument logged under the old serial number prints a bold \"BRIDGE PENDING\" annotation in the bulletin header so a reader never mistakes a one-week instrumental artifact for a neighbourhood-scale shift.",
